---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import AffiliateDisclosure from '../../../components/AffiliateDisclosure.astro';
import SourceBlock from '../../../components/SourceBlock.astro';

const title = 'Systeme.io vs Kajabi (2026): Price, Courses & Marketing Compared';
const description = 'Systeme.io vs Kajabi compared on pricing, course delivery, email, funnels, automation, selling and the business models each platform fits best.';
const canonical = '/compare/systeme-io-vs-kajabi/';
const aff = 'https://systeme.io/?sa=sa0279351357ea4e5af663df944162ce92d26d43e5';
const schema = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Article', headline: title, description, datePublished: '2026-09-12', dateModified: '2026-09-15' };
const sources = [
  { title: 'Systeme.io Pricing', url: 'https://systeme.io/pricing', publisher: 'Systeme.io', accessedAt: new Date('2026-09-15'), supports: 'Current monthly plan prices, contact limits, funnel/course limits, unlimited email sends and transaction-fee policy.' },
  { title: 'Systeme.io Features', url: 'https://systeme.io/features', publisher: 'Systeme.io', accessedAt: new Date('2026-09-15'), supports: 'Funnels, email marketing, automation, courses, communities, affiliate management and related platform capabilities.' },
  { title: 'Kajabi Pricing', url: 'https://www.kajabi.com/pricing', publisher: 'Kajabi', accessedAt: new Date('2026-09-15'), supports: 'Current monthly Basic, Growth and Pro pricing plus product, contact, website, community and admin-user limits.' },
  { title: 'Kajabi Online Courses', url: 'https://www.kajabi.com/product/online-courses', publisher: 'Kajabi', accessedAt: new Date('2026-09-15'), supports: 'Course delivery, email and automation integration, funnels, checkout, upsells, order bumps and affiliate capabilities.' },
  { title: 'Kajabi Funnels', url: 'https://www.kajabi.com/features/funnels', publisher: 'Kajabi', accessedAt: new Date('2026-09-15'), supports: 'Ready-made funnels, lead capture, product launches and integrated campaign structure.' },
  { title: 'Kajabi Email Marketing', url: 'https://www.kajabi.com/features/email-marketing', publisher: 'Kajabi', accessedAt: new Date('2026-09-15'), supports: 'Kajabi email, landing page, checkout, automation and analytics positioning for knowledge businesses.' }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} schema={schema}>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Compare', href: '/compare/' }, { label: 'Systeme.io vs Kajabi' }]} />
  <article>
    <header class="section"><div class="container"><h1>Systeme.io vs Kajabi: The Better Choice Depends on What You Are Building</h1><p class="lede">Systeme.io is dramatically cheaper. Kajabi is built around a more expensive knowledge-business stack. The useful comparison is not “which one has more features?” but which one fits the economics and operating model of your business.</p></div></header>
    <div class="narrow prose article-body">
      <AffiliateDisclosure hasRelationship={true} />
      <p>Systeme.io and Kajabi overlap enough to look interchangeable on a feature checklist. Both can support courses, landing pages, funnels, email marketing, automation and online selling. But the overlap hides an important difference: Systeme.io is positioned as a low-cost all-in-one operating stack, while Kajabi is positioned around experts and knowledge businesses that want creation, marketing, selling and client delivery under one roof.</p>
      <p>That distinction matters because the price gap is large. Systeme.io currently lists Free, Startup, Webinar and Unlimited plans at $0, $17, $47 and $97 per month. Kajabi lists Basic, Growth and Pro at $179, $249 and $499 per month on monthly billing. A buyer should therefore ask whether Kajabi's business-model fit and product environment justify the extra recurring cost rather than comparing entry prices in isolation.</p>

      <h2>Quick verdict</h2>
      <p><strong>Choose Systeme.io</strong> if you want to launch or consolidate funnels, email, automation, courses and affiliate management at the lowest practical software cost.</p>
      <p><strong>Choose Kajabi</strong> if courses, coaching, memberships, communities or other knowledge products are the center of the business and you are willing to pay substantially more for a platform designed around that operating model.</p>
      <p>Neither conclusion means one platform is universally better. A mature course business can rationally prefer Kajabi even when Systeme.io is cheaper. A solopreneur can rationally prefer Systeme.io even when Kajabi has a more specialized knowledge-business positioning.</p>

      <h2>Systeme.io vs Kajabi at a glance</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Decision factor</th><th>Systeme.io</th><th>Kajabi</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Starting monthly price</td><td>Free plan</td><td>$179/month Basic</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Paid monthly range compared here</td><td>$17 to $97</td><td>$179 to $499</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Free plan</td><td>Yes</td><td>No permanent free plan shown on current pricing page</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Funnels</td><td>Included</td><td>Included</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Email marketing</td><td>Included; unlimited sends are listed across plans</td><td>Included</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Courses</td><td>Included; Free plan includes one course</td><td>Core product category</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Automation</td><td>Integrated across the stack</td><td>Integrated across marketing and product workflows</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Affiliate management</td><td>Included across plans</td><td>Available as part of the selling stack</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Best-fit buyer</td><td>Cost-sensitive creator or small online business wanting one stack</td><td>Knowledge business prioritizing course/coaching/community delivery</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>

      <h2>Pricing: the gap is not subtle</h2>
      <p>Systeme.io's current pricing page says its Free plan supports 2,000 contacts, three sales funnels and one course. Startup is $17 per month with 5,000 contacts, unlimited funnels and five courses. Webinar is $47 per month with 10,000 contacts, while Unlimited is $97 per month with unlimited contacts. Systeme.io also states that all plans include unlimited email sends and no platform transaction fees.</p>
      <p>Kajabi's current pricing page lists Basic at $179 per month, Growth at $249 and Pro at $499 on monthly billing. Basic includes five products, 2,500 contacts, one website, one community and two admin users. Growth expands to 50 products and 25,000 contacts, while Pro lists unlimited products, 100,000 contacts, three websites and three communities.</p>
      <p>That makes the initial economics very different. Systeme.io lets a new creator test a complete workflow without a monthly software bill. Kajabi asks the buyer to start from a materially higher fixed cost. The higher price can still make sense if Kajabi replaces several tools and its product workflow is central to revenue, but the business should earn that decision rather than assume “premium” automatically means “better.”</p>

      <h2>Courses and knowledge products</h2>
      <p>This is where the comparison becomes more interesting than price. Systeme.io includes an online-course builder inside the same environment as funnels, email and automation. Its official features page describes video lessons, downloadable files, drip scheduling, module locking and student-progress tracking, with course access connected to purchase and automation workflows.</p>
      <p>Kajabi goes further in how it positions the entire platform: courses, coaching programs, memberships, communities and other knowledge-based offers are not side features; they are the center of the product story. Kajabi's course material emphasizes connected delivery, email, automations, funnels, checkout, upsells, order bumps and affiliate tools around the student/customer lifecycle.</p>
      <p>So the decision is not simply “both host courses.” If you primarily need a course to sit inside a broader low-cost marketing stack, Systeme.io has a strong economic case. If the learning product, client experience and knowledge-business workflow are the core of the company, Kajabi's specialization may be more valuable.</p>

      <h2>Email marketing and automation</h2>
      <p>Both platforms try to remove the need to stitch together separate email and automation tools. Systeme.io says its email marketing includes broadcasts, automated sequences, tagging and segmentation, and that the list is connected directly to funnels, courses and products. Its automation model links triggers, conditions and actions across those parts of the platform.</p>
      <p>Kajabi likewise connects email, landing pages, checkout and automation to products and customer activity. Its course material specifically describes automations responding to events such as a purchase, module completion or inactivity. For a knowledge business, that can make follow-up part of the product-delivery workflow instead of a separate marketing system.</p>
      <p>For most buyers the practical question is therefore not “does it automate?” Both do. The question is whether you prefer Systeme.io's lower-cost general all-in-one approach or Kajabi's more knowledge-business-centered environment.</p>

      <h2>Funnels and selling</h2>
      <p>Systeme.io's funnel builder supports opt-in, sales and webinar funnels and integrates with email, checkout and automation. Its feature page also lists A/B testing, upsells, order bumps and countdown timers.</p>
      <p>Kajabi's funnel product emphasizes ready-made campaign structures for lead generation, launches and list growth, with pages, copy and emails assembled into a connected campaign. Kajabi also integrates checkout and post-purchase product delivery inside the same system.</p>
      <p>If funnels are the main job and you want to minimize software cost, Systeme.io is difficult to ignore. If funnels are mainly the acquisition layer for a higher-value course, coaching or membership business, Kajabi may fit the surrounding workflow better.</p>

      <h2>Contact and product limits change the real price</h2>
      <p>Comparisons become misleading when they stop at headline subscription prices. The right comparison uses the plan that supports your actual list size, number of products, funnels, courses, websites and team.</p>
      <p>For example, Kajabi Basic's 2,500-contact allowance is close to Systeme.io Free's 2,000-contact allowance, but the monthly prices are obviously not close. On the other hand, a Kajabi buyer may be paying for a specialized product environment rather than just contact capacity. Likewise, a Systeme.io buyer should not assume the cheapest plan will remain sufficient as the number of courses, funnels or contacts grows.</p>

      <h2>Who should choose Systeme.io?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>You are validating an offer and want a real free starting point.</li><li>You want funnels, email, automation and courses in one inexpensive stack.</li><li>Your priority is reducing recurring software cost and tool sprawl.</li><li>You run a small creator business, affiliate operation or digital-product business where course delivery is important but not the entire operating model.</li><li>You prefer to spend budget on traffic, content or product development before committing hundreds of dollars per month to the platform layer.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who should choose Kajabi?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Your business is fundamentally built around courses, coaching, memberships, communities or expert-led programs.</li><li>You want marketing and customer delivery organized around knowledge products from the beginning.</li><li>The platform cost is small relative to the revenue and margin of the offer you sell.</li><li>You value a purpose-built knowledge-business environment more than minimizing monthly software spend.</li><li>You are evaluating the platform as an operating system for an established business rather than simply as the cheapest way to launch a funnel.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Migration and switching cost matter</h2>
      <p>A platform decision is not only a subscription-price decision. Moving courses, contacts, automations, pages, domains, checkout flows and email sequences has a real cost in time and risk. A business already deeply configured in Kajabi should not switch simply because Systeme.io is cheaper. Likewise, a Systeme.io user should not move to Kajabi unless the expected workflow or revenue benefit is large enough to justify both the higher subscription and the migration work.</p>

      <h2>Systeme.io vs Kajabi: our decision framework</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>What is the product?</strong> A simple digital offer and funnel stack points more naturally toward Systeme.io; a knowledge-business ecosystem gives Kajabi a stronger case.</li>
        <li><strong>What capacity do you need now?</strong> Compare the actual plan required for your contacts, products, courses and team.</li>
        <li><strong>Which workflow creates revenue?</strong> Do not pay for specialized product infrastructure you will not use, but do not choose the cheapest stack if it creates friction in the part of the business that actually makes money.</li>
        <li><strong>What is the switching cost?</strong> Include migration effort and operational risk, not just monthly subscription fees.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Final verdict</h2>
      <p>For a new or cost-sensitive creator, <strong>Systeme.io is the stronger starting point</strong>. The free tier and $17 paid entry point let you build a legitimate funnel, email and course workflow before software cost becomes a serious line item.</p>
      <p>For an established knowledge business, <strong>Kajabi remains a credible premium choice</strong>. Its value case is not lower price; it is the concentration of course, coaching, community, marketing and selling workflows around an expert-led business model.</p>
      <p>If your main question is “How do I launch and consolidate my stack cheaply?”, start with Systeme.io. If the question is “What platform do I want at the center of a mature knowledge business?”, compare Kajabi on workflow and product fit rather than price alone.</p>
      <p><a class="button" href={aff} rel="sponsored nofollow">Try Systeme.io free</a></p>
      <SourceBlock sources={sources} />
      <p class="source-note">Facts checked September 15, 2026 against official Systeme.io and Kajabi pages. Pricing and limits can change. We did not hands-on test every capability described here; feature claims above are limited to what the vendors currently document. “Best fit” judgments are SelectVerdict editorial conclusions, not measured conversion claims.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
